\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces  (a) The Cram\'er-Rao derived relative uncertainty for a range of correlated and uncorrelated error ratios. The thin lines correspond to expected uncertainties using $\langle Z\rangle $ measurements from \emph  {k-copy} RB, while thick lines use both $\langle Z\rangle $ and parity measurements, as described in section~\ref {sec:parity}. (b-c) The required number of shots, $N$, per sequence length, $m$, for unity fractional uncertainty, for a range of correlated and uncorrelated errors. In (b), only $\langle Z\rangle $ measurements are used, while in (c) both $\langle Z\rangle $ and parity measurements are used. A contour is shown for $5000$ samples, which is typically used in our experiments. }}{100}{figure.caption.49}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch4_fisher_2}{{5.6}{100}{(a) The Cram\'er-Rao derived relative uncertainty for a range of correlated and uncorrelated error ratios. The thin lines correspond to expected uncertainties using $\langle Z\rangle $ measurements from \emph {k-copy} RB, while thick lines use both $\langle Z\rangle $ and parity measurements, as described in section~\ref {sec:parity}. (b-c) The required number of shots, $N$, per sequence length, $m$, for unity fractional uncertainty, for a range of correlated and uncorrelated errors. In (b), only $\langle Z\rangle $ measurements are used, while in (c) both $\langle Z\rangle $ and parity measurements are used. A contour is shown for $5000$ samples, which is typically used in our experiments}{figure.caption.49}{}}
